## CPU autoscaling

Autoscaling uses Kubernetes HPA with Pod CPU utilization measured against CPU requests. Install and maintain a compatible Metrics Server in the target Kubernetes cluster before enabling autoscaling. The worker metrics exporter and Prometheus Remote Write are separate from Metrics Server and are not prerequisites for HPA.

Set both CPU policies to the same integer from 1 to 100. They express one target CPU utilization, not separate scale-in and scale-out thresholds. Both policies are required; the Provider does not supply a default. Worker bounds must satisfy `1 <= min_worker_count <= max_worker_count`. Worker counts must fit the API's signed 32-bit integer type; no additional capacity limit is imposed by the Provider.

AutoMQ checks Metrics API access when enabling HPA. Customers maintain Metrics Server, node capacity, periodic sampling, and alerts. HPA scales worker Pods; it does not increase connector `tasks.max` or provision additional Kubernetes nodes.

The default HPA behavior allows up to two additional workers every 15 seconds with no scale-up stabilization window, and up to one fewer worker every 60 seconds with a 300-second scale-down stabilization window. Lowering `max_worker_count` can cause immediate scale-in to the new bound.

Capacity mode and policy changes update the existing cluster in place. To switch to fixed capacity, replace the whole `capacity` object:

```terraform
capacity = {
  type = "provisioned"
  provisioned = {
    worker_resource_spec = "TIER2"
    worker_count         = 4
  }
}
```

Only the capacity object selected by `type` may be present. Read and import use the saved capacity configuration. HPA replica changes do not change Terraform state or require `ignore_changes`.

